WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.347 --> 00:00:04.117
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.117 --> 00:00:13.435
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; 13 years ago,

00:00:13.435 --> 00:00:17.289
it was my freshman year of college, 2006.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:22.565
And I was attending my first meeting of
the Association of Black Harvard women.

00:00:22.565 --> 00:00:27.920
I was so excited to meet fellow black
women classmates for the first time.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.738
We went around in a circle
introducing ourselves.

00:00:32.738 --> 00:00:36.512
The first woman to go happened
to have a Nigerian name.

00:00:36.512 --> 00:00:39.836
She said, hi, my name's Adora Escunananay,

00:00:39.836 --> 00:00:43.088
it mean's a child who is
one of the community.

00:00:43.088 --> 00:00:46.949
Who is given to all and
whom all must look after.

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:50.134
Then, the second girl went.

00:00:50.134 --> 00:00:54.752
She said hi, my name is Suran Al Alazah,
it means a sacred flower,

00:00:54.752 --> 00:00:58.220
one found in the Bible
that is especially sacred.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:03.727
Then it was my turn, I said,
hi, my name is Mercedes Bent.

00:01:03.727 --> 00:01:08.148
It means, well,
it's kind of just like the car.

00:01:08.148 --> 00:01:13.021
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That moment seeded a curiosity in

00:01:13.021 --> 00:01:14.932
me about African names.

00:01:14.932 --> 00:01:19.975
I was so
interested in the beauty of the names.

00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:24.426
12 years later, I would find
myself leading a trip to Ghana and

00:01:24.426 --> 00:01:26.240
Nigeria with Stanford and

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.696
Harvard classmates where we were going
to explore our heritage and our roots.

00:01:31.696 --> 00:01:36.013
I had started calling
the trip my black birthright.

00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:40.651
When I think back on what
inspired me to plan such a trip,

00:01:40.651 --> 00:01:46.787
I think back to that day my freshman
year of college and to two other times.

00:01:46.787 --> 00:01:52.002
The second time was also in
college when I first learned about

00:01:52.002 --> 00:01:57.126
the Jewish birthright or
[FOREIGN] birthright Israel trip.

00:01:57.126 --> 00:02:01.862
My friends in college told me about this
all expenses paid trip where they got to

00:02:01.862 --> 00:02:06.384
go visit Israel, learn about their
heritage, connect to their people and

00:02:06.384 --> 00:02:09.789
get really indoctrinated in
what it meant to be Jewish.

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:14.234
I was like, that's so
cool why don't we have that?

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:19.106
The third incident that inspired the trip
was a few years later when I was in

00:02:19.106 --> 00:02:20.454
the working world.

00:02:20.454 --> 00:02:25.771
It was 2016 and I was sitting at
my desk one day, when my phone and

00:02:25.771 --> 00:02:29.801
computer started blowing
up with notifications.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:31.627
I clicked on a link.

00:02:31.627 --> 00:02:36.015
And what I saw next horrified me.

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:41.416
There was a video streaming on Facebook
Live of a man who had just been shot.

00:02:41.416 --> 00:02:42.310
His fiance or

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.762
a girlfriend was sitting next to him in
the side seat was filming the aftermath.

00:02:47.762 --> 00:02:52.282
Her four year old daughter
was in the backseat.

00:02:52.282 --> 00:02:57.373
They had just he had just been shot
after being pulled over by the police

00:02:57.373 --> 00:03:02.997
on a routine traffic stop and had been
shot about seven times in 40 seconds.

00:03:02.997 --> 00:03:05.200
My mind started raising.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.268
I started thinking,
why did someone shoot into the car so

00:03:09.268 --> 00:03:12.507
quickly right after
a routine traffic stop?

00:03:12.507 --> 00:03:13.907
Why would someone shoot into the car?

00:03:13.907 --> 00:03:17.609
There was a little girl in the back seat.

00:03:17.609 --> 00:03:22.989
What if it had been my dad or
brother in the front seat?

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:28.334
What if I had been
the little girl in the back?

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:32.536
His name was Philando Castile.

00:03:32.536 --> 00:03:37.285
That was the incident that
jolted me very deeply.

00:03:37.285 --> 00:03:42.074
The rest of the day at work, I tried
to focus on getting things done and

00:03:42.074 --> 00:03:44.811
being a productive member of the team.

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:49.563
But unfortunately, my teammates didn't
get anything from me the rest of the day.

00:03:49.563 --> 00:03:52.444
I couldn't focus.

00:03:52.444 --> 00:03:56.708
Later that week, I called my friend and
I said we've gotta do something.

00:03:56.708 --> 00:03:58.959
We've gotta help black people.

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:03.401
We looked into how we could support
economic empowerment initiatives or

00:04:03.401 --> 00:04:05.962
how we could support Black Lives Matter.

00:04:05.962 --> 00:04:09.758
We donated and rallied,
but nothing quite stuck.

00:04:09.758 --> 00:04:13.892
We were still yearning to do more.

00:04:13.892 --> 00:04:17.824
It would take another two years before
I found a solution that did stick.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:22.451
And it ended up being my trip to Ghana and
Nigeria.

00:04:22.451 --> 00:04:26.468
I didn't realize it at the time
that it would become a solution.

00:04:26.468 --> 00:04:30.287
But it did end up being one, in a way.

00:04:30.287 --> 00:04:33.879
The trip itself was full of new and
exciting adventures.

00:04:33.879 --> 00:04:38.756
We went to concerts where we
danced all night to Afro beats.

00:04:38.756 --> 00:04:41.134
I learned how to cook jollof rice,

00:04:41.134 --> 00:04:46.227
a dish that is a staple of African
diets and has inspired intense rivalry.

00:04:46.227 --> 00:04:51.107
I got my hair braided, we discussed
Nigerian politics and history.

00:04:51.107 --> 00:04:53.846
And I even got a new name.

00:04:53.846 --> 00:04:58.705
So I participated in
a traditional Nigerian naming

00:04:58.705 --> 00:05:02.101
ceremony where they gave me a name.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:05.718
Assosa Adowa Olakay,
Assosa means God's gift.

00:05:05.718 --> 00:05:07.761
Adowa means born on a Monday.

00:05:07.761 --> 00:05:11.152
And Olakay is the family name.

00:05:11.152 --> 00:05:16.179
They said to me, you will always
have a family here in Nigeria.

00:05:16.179 --> 00:05:18.971
Welcome to the family.

00:05:18.971 --> 00:05:23.652
For the first time in my life, I felt
a surge of gratitude and appreciation as I

00:05:23.652 --> 00:05:28.188
realized I finally felt connected to
the people of the African continent,

00:05:28.188 --> 00:05:29.064
to my people.

00:05:32.078 --> 00:05:38.913
Over the course of the trip, I realized
that my mind was really being put at ease.

00:05:38.913 --> 00:05:43.169
That the recurring racial soundtrack
caused by microaggressions

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:46.059
in the United States
was starting to lessen.

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:52.477
That I was healing mentally,
that I was healing through heritage.

00:05:55.193 --> 00:05:59.885
Over the course of that trip
I also thought about well,

00:05:59.885 --> 00:06:03.358
what if I lived here in Ghana or Nigeria?

00:06:03.358 --> 00:06:08.175
I wouldn't be subjected to as many
racially charged microaggressions and

00:06:08.175 --> 00:06:12.999
I wouldn't have to wonder if I was hired
for my token, or for my potential.

00:06:12.999 --> 00:06:17.796
Research shows that the development of
ethnic and racial identity can have really

00:06:17.796 --> 00:06:21.424
positive benefits for
a minoritized youth and young adults.

00:06:21.424 --> 00:06:25.388
These positive benefits can
include higher self-esteem,

00:06:25.388 --> 00:06:29.984
greater confidence and even higher
academic achievement in school.

00:06:29.984 --> 00:06:35.326
Research has also shown that
in interventions rooted

00:06:35.326 --> 00:06:40.310
in indigenous traditions,
have had the effect of

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:45.783
decreasing substance abuse and
attempts at suicide.

00:06:45.783 --> 00:06:48.774
So what I'm talking about here,
the developing an ethnic and

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:51.247
racial identity and
connecting to your heritage,

00:06:51.247 --> 00:06:55.232
is not just a matter of having higher
self-esteem or having greater confidence.

00:06:55.232 --> 00:06:59.782
Although those could be great benefits,

00:06:59.782 --> 00:07:03.555
it can be a matter of life or death.

00:07:03.555 --> 00:07:08.639
I strongly believe that anybody,
any displaced person who has been

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:14.347
displaced whether due to freeing for
a new country or simply immigrating for

00:07:14.347 --> 00:07:19.699
a better life, can benefit from
planning their own birthright trip and

00:07:19.699 --> 00:07:22.670
finding some of the healing that I did.

00:07:25.345 --> 00:07:28.823
So where do I go from here?

00:07:28.823 --> 00:07:32.754
As I said, thought about,
if I lived in the Ghana or Nigeria,

00:07:32.754 --> 00:07:36.762
some of the healing benefits I would get,
but if I lived there,

00:07:36.762 --> 00:07:39.964
I wouldn't get to live
here in the United States.

00:07:39.964 --> 00:07:44.187
Where we have the greatest melting
pot experiment going on that

00:07:44.187 --> 00:07:45.867
the world's ever seen.

00:07:45.867 --> 00:07:50.871
Call me an optimist, but
I still share Martin Luther King's

00:07:50.871 --> 00:07:56.179
dream that one day, we won't be
judged by the color of our skin.

00:07:56.179 --> 00:07:59.377
I'm committed to helping
more young adults,

00:07:59.377 --> 00:08:04.800
more youth of minoritized backgrounds,
go on trips, such as the one is I did.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:08.332
I've gotten involved with an organization
called Birthright AFRICA.

00:08:08.332 --> 00:08:13.271
And I'm hoping that together we
can help them raise money to send

00:08:13.271 --> 00:08:15.470
more people on these trips.

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:19.534
I hope that in sharing my
story today with you guys,

00:08:19.534 --> 00:08:23.409
a short story of the healing
that I went through,

00:08:23.409 --> 00:08:28.325
that you're either inspired to
go find your own birthright or

00:08:28.325 --> 00:08:31.274
support those of us on this journey.

00:08:31.274 --> 00:08:31.921
Thank you.

00:08:31.921 --> 00:08:36.846
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:36.846 --> 00:08:39.846
[MUSIC]

